\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Covering arcs meet the length of the circle}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Let a circle of length #{1} be covered by countably many
closed arcs, of lengths #{\ell_i}. The sum of the lengths is
at least #{1}.}

\proof{
\p{If some length is at least #{1}, the sum is at least #{1}.
Assume, on the contrary, that the sum is strictly less than
#{1}. Every length is then strictly less than #{1}. Let
#{\varepsilon} be half the difference between #{1} and the
sum, and widen the #{i}-th arc by extending each end. The new
arc is open, and its length is the old length plus
#{\varepsilon 2^{-(i+1)}}. The new lengths still sum to less
than #{1}, and the open arcs cover the circle. Compactness
retains a finite subcover. Every arc in that subcover still
has length strictly less than #{1}.}

\p{Cut the circle at one point and open the cut onto the unit
interval. An open arc from the finite subcover lifts to one
open interval, or to two open intervals when the arc contains
the cut. The lifted lengths add to the arc lengths, so their
sum is strictly less than #{1}. The lifted intervals cover
the compact interval running from #{\eta} to #{1-\eta}, for
every #{\eta} with #{0<\eta<1/2}.}

\p{Start at the left endpoint of that compact interval. Among
the open intervals that contain the current point, choose one
whose right endpoint is maximal. The chosen interval is open,
so the right endpoint lies strictly beyond the current point,
and the length of the interval is at least the distance
advanced. Continue from that right endpoint. The right
endpoints strictly increase, so each interval is chosen at
most once, and a finite subcover forces the process to pass
the right end of the compact interval. The chosen lengths
therefore sum to at least #{1-2\eta}. The lifted total is at
least #{1-2\eta}. Letting #{\eta} tend to #{0} makes that
total at least #{1}, contradicting the widening. The original
sum is therefore at least #{1}.}
}
